 @Soulleey  Pretty sure they were talking about GPU memory. When you max out your VRAM, the GPU needs to park some data into normal RAM every frame and then load it back onto the GPU every frame, which runs at about 20GB/s as opposed to the 500GB/s you get from direct VRAM access. This is why the FPS drops by factor 20. The actual performance difference between using a 128 and 256 shader pack is minimal if it can fit into VRAM.

Additionally, I bet the actual GPU usage was much higher when using the 128 pack because of this. While the GPU has to wait for resources to be moved around, it's not spending that time doing work. GPU usage is increased by complexity of shaders, not texture size.

Additionally, you don't really need to do anything at all to max out a GPU. You just need to have the CPU supply commands to the GPU faster than it can execute them. This can happen even in base Minecraft with a fast CPU and integrated graphics. Especially if you turn your render distance up. Though for a 3090 Ti, you'd need an insanely fast CPU and memory as in unmodded Minecraft, the render commands are quite small and lightweight.

 @abubakrakram6208  Unified memory is very slow. The only way to get GPU usage up on such systems is to keep the memory usage down (low res texture pack) and swamp the cores with computations (sine, cosine, square roots, logarithm, all very good options. You want shaders that use these functions extensively while only using very few textures). You need to balance the resolution, as it will massively increase the computations needed, but at the cost of increasing memory usage. There should be a sweet spot.

In game, stand in a location that is fully surrounded by transparent blocks (that don't block the rendering of over transparent blocks), slap loads of armor stands and the like everywhere, or a 3d "checkerboard" pattern of solid blocks and glass. How well that works depends on the shaders - If your shaders support realistic shadows, this will massively destroy performance (increase GPU load) though it will also increase memory usage so it might throttle there.

Really it depends on your shaders whether you can get near 100% or not. You really want some that do tons of complicated computations with minimal data.

